## ABSTRACT
Endo/lysosomal escape and gene release are two critical bottlenecks in gene delivery. Herein, a novel photo-controllable metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) nanoswitch is rationally designed for enhancing small interfering RNA (siRNA) delivery. One single laser triggers the "off-to-on" switching of MOFs nanocomplexes, inducing significant siRNA release accompanied by rapid MOFs dissociation into protonatable 2-methylimidazalo and osmotic rupturing \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) ions, which cooperatively contribute to remarkable endo/lysosomal rupture \((\sim90\%)\). The simultaneous endo/lysosomal rupture and release enable a high spatio-temporal control on RNA interference for effective cancer therapy. Notably, the "off-to-on" switching also activates fluorescence recovery for real-time monitoring siRNA delivery. The nanoswitch could easily be extended to deliver other therapeutic agents (e.g., DNA, protein, anticancer drug) for overcoming endo/lysosomal entrapment.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, endo/lysosomal rupture and release, photo-controllable nanoswitch, RNA interference, cancer therapy
## 1 Introduction
The success of RNA interference (RNAi) highly depends on gene vectors, which mainly undergo endocytosis resulting in endo/lysosomal trafficking where unwanted enzymatic degradation of genetic material occurs [1–3]. To realize RNAi, gene vectors need to induce endo/lysosomal rupture and release the genetic information into cytoplasm [4–7]. However, conventional gene vectors (e.g., cationic lipid and polymeric based ones) generally fail to achieve this aim due to the challenge in simultaneously satisfying desirable endo/lysosomal rupture and effective cargo release [2, 8, 9]. One representative example is polyethyleneimine (PEI) based ones that usually suffers from slow and inefficient release as well as potential toxicity, despite its confirmed endo/lysosomal disruption by "proton sponge effect" [10–13]. There is an imperative need for developing novel vectors for concurrently addressing efficient endo/lysosomal escape and gene release.
Recently, light-assisted endo/lysosomal disruption has attracted much interest owing to its high spatial-temporal controllability
Recently, light-assisted endo/lysosomal disruption has attracted much interest owing to its high spatial-temporal controllability [14–17]. However, the mainstream vectors for gene delivery usually involve cationic polymers for nucleic acid packaging and/or endo/lysosomal disruption [18–20], thus fail to fully get rid of the aforementioned dilemma of conventional vectors as well as potential non-specific gene transfer. Herein, we rationally designed a photo-controllable metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) nanoswitch to induce simultaneous endo/lysosomal rupture and gene release for enhancing small interfering RNA (siRNA) delivery and cancer RNAi. The MOFs nanoswitch consists of zinc-based MOFs, zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) nanoparticles containing photothermal agents indocyanine green (ICG) inside with siRNAs wrapped through electrostatic adsorption (Scheme 1). Upon mild photoirradiation, internal light-harvesting ICG generates local heat to achieve the "off-to-on" switching, rapidly releasing a large amount of siRNAs and dissociating the pH-sensitive ZIF-8 into \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) and 2-methylimidazolate (MI) in the acidic endo/lysosome. Due to the osmotic rupturing of \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) and pH-buffering of MI [21, 22], we propose and validate that this "off-to-on" switching is able to break the endo/lysosome in a photo-controlled manner.
Such switching offers a high spatio-temporal control over gene transfer for cancer site-specific RNAi. Notably, recovery in ICG fluorescence (FL) signals during the off-to-on switching period could be employed to monitor the siRNA delivery in real-time.
## 2 Results and discussion
