We expect that the internal ICG could convert light into heat and promote the breakdown of the coordination between MI and \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) in acidic medium, and thus rapidly dissociates the ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA nanoswitch into siRNA, \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) and MI (Fig. 1(a)) [23, 24]. We irradiated ZIF-8 and ICG@ZIF-8 nanoparticle solutions with an infrared laser (808 nm, \( 0.75 \, \mathrm{W/cm}^{2} \)). The ZIF-8 samples exhibited a negligible temperature increase close to that of water (Fig. S8 in the ESM), while the temperature of the ICG@ZIF-8 nanoparticles steadily increased to \( 47.8 \, ^{\circ} \mathrm{C} \) within \( 5 \, \mathrm{min} \) of photoirradiation (Fig. 1(b)), suggesting the occurrence of ICG-mediated light-to-heat conversion. Subsequently, the dissociation of ICG@ZIF-8 was examined by monitoring the morphology of nanoparticles after different treatments using TEM. Figure 1(c) shows that the ICG@ZIF-8 maintained their morphology at \( \mathrm{pH} \, 7.4 \), but slightly dissociated either at \( \mathrm{pH} \, 5.5 \) or after photoirradiation treatment. However, the combination of photoirradiation and acidic \( \mathrm{(pH} \, 5.5) \) intensified the most drastic disassociation and almost all ZIF-8 dissociated completely within \( 5 \, \mathrm{min} \), which was in agreement with DLS data (Fig. S9 in the ESM), confirming the accelerated disassociation by the generated heat from irradiated ICG.
To control the photo-induced dissociation, we irradiated the MOFs nanoswitch with varying periods (0, 3 and \( 5 \, \mathrm{min} \)) of photoirradiation and then quantified the release of siRNA and \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) using agarose gel electrophoresis assay and inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analysis, respectively. A \( \mathrm{pH} \, 5.5 \) sodium acetate buffer \( \left( 10 \, \mathrm{mM} \right) \) was used to mimic the acidic environment of endo/lysosome. As expected, the nanoswitch only released a small amount \( (\sim 5\%) \) of siRNA in the dark, but released \( 20\% \) and \( 55\% \) of siRNA after 3 and \( 5 \, \mathrm{min} \) photoirradiation, respectively (Fig. 1(d)). Consistent with siRNA release, the \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) release also increased from \( 20\% \) to \( 37\% \) and \( 66\% \) after 3 and \( 5 \, \mathrm{min} \) of photoirradiation, respectively (Fig. 1(e)). Since \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) is the building block of ZIF-8, the steady increase in release of siRNAs and \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) with extended irradiation time confirmed the feasibility of controlling the nanoswitch dissociation by photoirradiation. Besides, the ICG@ZIF-8 fluorescence signals remained low at \( \mathrm{pH} \, 7.4 \), which was possibly due to the ICG self-quenching effect, also increased significantly after photoirradiation in \( \mathrm{pH} \, 5.5 \) (Fig. 1(f)) that supports the photocontrolled nanoswitch dissociation. The recovery of fluorescence offers a great way for real-time monitoring the siRNA delivery and release.
We hypothesize that the photo-controlled nanoswitch dissociation is able to induce successive endo/lysosomal rupture and siRNA release into the cytoplasm due to the excellent pH-buffering of MI and osmotic rupturing of \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) ions (Fig. 2(a)) [21, 22, 25]. To confirm the hypothesis, the intracellular disassociation of the nanoswitch in A549 cancer cells was monitored using a \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \)-selective indicator (FluoZin™-3, green fluorescence) under confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM). Consistent with that in water, the MOFs nanoswitch in dark exhibited slow disassociation kinetics in acidic endo/lysosome and presented only 1.2-fold higher intracellular \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) concentration than the control cells, while the irradiated nanoswitch showed significant increased dissociation, displaying 5.4- and 16.6-fold higher amounts of \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) in A549 cells after 3 and \( 5 \, \mathrm{min} \) laser (Fig. 2(b) and Fig. S10 in the ESM), indicating the accelerated intracellular dissociation by photoirradiation.
Subsequently, the endo/lysosomal rupture was probed using acridine orange (AO) as an indicator, since it emits red fluorescence in endo/lysosomes but green fluorescence in the cytosol and nucleus. After different treatments, the A549 cells
were incubated with AO and then observed under CLSM to detect the endo/lysosome damage. Either A549 cells treated with the MOFs nanoswitch or incubated under mild hyperthermia \((42\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) showed strong red fluorescence of AO close to that in \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 2(c) and Fig. S11 in the ESM), suggesting that the heat or MOFs nanoswitch alone failed to cause endo/lysosomal rupture. Conversely, cells treated with the nanoswitch followed by photoirradiation presented significant decreased intensity of red fluorescence. The longer irradiation time resulted in the less observed red fluorescence, suggesting increased endo/lysosomal rupture accompanied with the increased nanoswitch dissociation by photoirradiation. The red fluorescence intensity decreased by \(90\%\) after \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) of photoirradiation compared with the control cells (Fig. S12 in the ESM), indicating large depletion of endo/lysosome. We infer this effective endo/lysosomal rupture from the large amount of released \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) and MI. The protonable MI could effectively counterbalance the protons in endo/lysosome and lead to additional pumping of protons (thus chloride ions), along with the released \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) ions, rapidly increasing the osmotic pressure and ultimately causing endo/lysosome swelling and rupture [21, 22, 25].
Besides endo/lysosomal escape, effective siRNA release is another critical prerequisite for successful RNAi [4, 26]. The siRNA intracellular release and localization were monitored using siRNA with carboxyfluorescein (FAM, green fluorescence) tags \(\left(\mathrm{siRNA}_{\mathrm{FAM}}\right)\). After condensed onto the MOFs nanoswitch,
the fluorescence of \(\mathrm{siRNA}_{\mathrm{FAM}}\) decreased significantly due to its self-quenching effect. While free \(\mathrm{siRNA}_{\mathrm{FAM}}\)-treated cells showed negligible green fluorescence (Fig. S13 in the ESM), the \(\mathrm{siRNA}_{\mathrm{FAM}}\) loaded nanoswitch presented significant increased intracellular green fluorescence (Fig. 3(a) and Fig. S14 in the ESM), indicating the enhanced cellular internalization of siRNA by the nanoswitch. However, the green fluorescence highly overlapped with the red fluorescence of the endo/lysosome tracker, suggesting the nanoswitch was mainly located in the endo/lysosome. After photoirradiation, the nanoswitch showed a significant increase in intracellular green fluorescence, suggesting increased siRNA release from the dissociated nanoswitch that de-quenched the FAM fluorescence. Concurrently, the green fluorescence gradually separated from the red fluorescence of the endo/lysosome tracker and spread to the cytoplasm. Colocalization analysis (Fig. 3(b)) was performed, showing that the colocalization ratio decreased from 0.97 to 0.50 and 0.17 after 3 and \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) of photoirradiation, respectively (Fig. 3(c)), indicating the success of photo-induced siRNA endo/lysosomal escape and release into cytoplasm.
Subsequently, the gene downregulation of the photocontrollable nanoswitch was explored by choosing Survivin siRNA (siSur) for targeting Survivin, a critical inhibitor of apoptosis protein [27, 28]. After incubation with the MOFs nanoswitch followed by photoirradiation \((808\,\mathrm{nm},\,0.75\,\mathrm{W/cm}^{2},\,10\,\mathrm{min})\), the protein in A549 cancer cells was harvested for
western blotting to detect Survivin abundance. As shown in Fig. 3(d), the siSur-loaded MOFs nanoswitch with laser exhibited significantly enhanced Survivin downregulation \((46\%)\) in A549 cells than that without laser \((15\%)\). Such photo-enhanced RNAi could be due to the nanoswitch-mediated effective endo/ lysosomal escape and siRNA release, both of which are essential rate-limiting steps in gene delivery.
Cell viability was further quantified using 3-(4,5- dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide (MTT) assays to verify the effect of gene downregulation, as Survivin reduction could cause cancer cell death [29]. Both bare MOFs (ICG@ZIF-8) and the control siRNA (siCtrl)-loaded MOFs nanoswitch showed negligible cytotoxicity to A549 cells, which was significantly lower than the commercial transfection agent PEI25K and their complexes with siCtrl (Fig. S15 in the ESM), suggesting the high biocompatibility of the MOFs nanoswitch. Consistent with the western blotting, the siSur loaded MOFs nanoswitch with photoirradiation presented significantly enhanced cell killing compared to that without photoirradiation (Fig. 3(e)). Such great enhancement in cell proliferation inhibition was in accordance with the photo-enhanced gene downregulation. The comparison between the additive index \((f_{\mathrm{additive}})\) and the combination index \((f_{\mathrm{combination}})\) shows that the \(f_{\mathrm{additive}}\) values gradually outnumbered \(f_{\mathrm{combination}}\) values with increased siRNA dose (Fig. 3(f) and Table S1 in the ESM), confirming the synergistic effect between photo and siRNA [30].
Given the highly enhanced in vitro RNAi, in vivo experiments were performed by injecting the MOFs or siSur-loaded MOFs nanoswitch intratumorally into A549 tumor models on nude mice. The illumination in the irradiated group was carefully controlled to induce mild hyperthermia \((\sim42\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) to trigger the "off-to-on" switching while preventing siRNA damage (Fig. 4(a) and Fig. S16 in the ESM) [16]. Meanwhile, the
delivery of siRNA was monitored under fluorescence imaging in a real-time way. Fluorescence signals of the tumor region increased immediately after the injection of MOFs nanoswitch, indicating the accumulation of nanoswitch in the tumor region; moreover, the fluorescence signals further increased after photoirradiation (Fig. 4(b)), confirming the occurrence of "off-to-on" switching that induces the endo/lysosomal escape and siRNA release by the photo-induced dissociation of the MOFs nanoswitch.
The tumor growth rate was recorded every two days to evaluate the in vivo therapeutic efficiency. As shown in Fig. 4(c), bare MOFs presented negligible inhibition of tumor growth compared to the phosphate buffered saline (PBS) control. The siSur-loaded MOFs nanoswitch exhibited a slight tumor inhibition \((19\%)\) closed to that of bare MOFs plus photoirradiation \((25\%)\), suggesting that either the nanoswitch-mediated RNAi in dark or mild hyperthermia was insufficient to inhibit tumor growth. However, the MOFs nanoswitch with photoirradiation showed a significantly enhanced tumor inhibition \((80\%)\), which was 3.3 times higher than that of the nanoswitch alone. At the end of therapy, mice were sacrificed and tumors tissues were harvested for hematoxylin/eosin (H&E) staining, showing much more cell death occurred in the nanoswitch/laser combination than other groups (Fig. 4(e)). The augmented tumor inhibition agreed with in vitro studies and validated the photo-enhanced cancer RNAi. Intratumoral injection was chose as the administered route in this study, due to its high efficiency and minimal side effect. The MOFs nanoswitch developed here is applicable for the treatment of easily accessible local tumors, but not suitable for metastatic tumor and local tumors in the tissues, which can only be reached through the systemic route.
To investigate the clearance of nanoswitch in vivo, the
concentrations of zinc in the major organs and tumor in the mice receiving the nanoswitch plus photoirradiation were analyzed by ICP. The zinc concentrations of the major organs and tumor decreased to the background levels at 14 days after the treatment (Fig. S17 in the ESM), suggesting the \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA}\) nanocomposites have been cleared from the body. All groups of mice had normal body weight growth (Fig. 4(d)), and no obvious damage was observed in the major organs (Fig. S18 in the ESM), demonstrating the minimal side effects of the nanoswitch. The good biosecurity of the MOFs nanoswitch probably came from the degradability of the MOFs that released zinc ions and MI, both of which are integral to biologicals; zinc is one of the most abundant metal elements in physiological systems, and the imidazole group presents an essential component of histidine [24].
## 3 Conclusion
We have developed a photo-controllable MOFs nanoswitch to break endo/lysosomes for enhancing cancer RNAi. A single laser achieved the "off-to-on" switching of the multifunctional MOFs nanoswitch, concurrently inducing endo/lysosomal rupture and gene release in a controlled manner, allowing a spatio-temporal control over gene delivery in targeted sites precisely, resulting in notably augmented cancer inhibition [31]. The fluorescence recovery during the switching offers a real-time method to monitor gene delivery and release. This type of MOFs nanoswitch could be applied as a general theranostic platform for overcoming multiply barriers in cytosolic delivery due to the versatility of ZIF-8 in loading different therapeutic agents (e.g., DNA, protein, anticancer drug) [24, 25, 32].
## 4 Experimental section
## 4.1 Material
\(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) 2-methylimidazole, AO, 4;6-diamidino-2- phenylindole (DAPI), MTT, diethyl pyrocarbonate (DEPC) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA). ICG was purchased from Sangon Biotech (Shanghai, China). The Lyso-Tracker Red was purchase from Beyotime (Shanghai, China). The FluoZin™-3 \((Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) indicator) was purchased from Thermo Fisher Scientific (USA). The surviving siRNAs were synthesized by Shanghai GenePharma Co. (China) using the previously reported sequence [33]. \(\upbeta\) -Actin and surviving antibodies were obtained from ImmunoWay Biotechnology, Inc. (USA). Penicillin–streptomycin, Dulbecco's modified Eagle medium (DMEM) and fetal bovine serum (FBS) were obtained from Gibco Life Technologies (USA).
## 4.2 Synthesis and characterization of \(\mathbf{ICG@ZIF-8}\) and ZIF-8
First, \(800~\mathrm{mg}\) of 2-methylimidazole was dissolved in a \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) mixed solution of water/methanol \((\mathrm{v/v}~=~1{:}1)\) ; meanwhile, ICG \((5~\mathrm{mg})\) was added into another \(10\,\mathrm{\mL}\) water/methanol \((\mathrm{v/v}~=~1{:}1)\) ) solution containing \(800~\mathrm{{\mg}~\mathrm{{\Zn}(\mathrm{{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}}}\) and incubated for \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\) . Then, the mixture containing \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and ICG were added to the 2-methylimidazole solution and stirred for \(3\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) nanoparticles were collected by centrifugal separation \((8,000~\mathrm{\rpm},~5~\mathrm{\min})\) and washed with a mixture of methanol and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) for three times and then stored in methanol before use.
The size, morphology and surface charge of \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) nanocomplexes were characterized by DLS (Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZS), TEM (FEI Tecnai F20) and zeta-potential (Malvern
The size, morphology and surface charge of \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) nanocomplexes were characterized by DLS (Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZS), TEM (FEI Tecnai F20) and zeta-potential (Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZS). The UV–vis absorption spectra of free ICG, ZIF-8 and \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) were measured on a SPECORD 250 PLUS UV–vis spectrophotometer (Analytik Jena, Germany). Photothermal heating ability of \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) nanoparticles was carried out by irradiating the samples of \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) nanoparticles with different ICG concentrations (0, 10, 25, 50 and \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL)}\) and equivalent ZIF-8 with laser \((808\,\mathrm{nm},0.75\,\mathrm{W/cm}^{2})\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) . The real-time temperature changes were recorded by a FLIR Ax5 thermal camera (MultiPix imaging, UK).
The ICG content in the \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) nanoparticles was determined by the following steps. The \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) was firstly fully disintegrated by incubating in \(1\ \mathrm{mL}\) HCl solution \(\left(\mathrm{pH}=2\right)\) . The ICG concentration was determined by the established ICG standard curve of UV–vis absorbance (Fig. S3 in the ESM). The drug encapsulation capability (EC) was calculated using the following equation
\(\mathrm{EC}\left(\%\right)=\) (weight of loaded ICG)/(weight of \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8})\times100\) .
## 4.3 Preparation of \(\mathbf{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNAs}\)
The binding ability of \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) was investigated by using agarose gel electrophoresis assay. The \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA}\) nanocomplexes with different ICG/siRNA weight ratios (e.g. 15, 30, 60, 120, 240) were prepared by mixing different amount of \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) nanoparticles with siRNA (1 pmol) in DEPC treated water. The resultant nanocomplexes were further incubated at room temperature (RT) for \(15~\mathrm{min}\) and then subjected to agarose gel electrophoresis assay.
## 4.4 Stability of siRNA in serum
Free siRNA or that complexed with \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) at the ICG/ siRNA ratio (w/w) of 60 was incubated with \(50\%\) serum for varied time period. Subsequently, the free siRNA was subjected to the agarose gel electrophoresis. The \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA}\) samples were treated with \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\) heparin and incubated for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) , and then was subjected to the agarose gel electrophoresis.
## 4.5 Photo-induce dissociation of \(\mathbf{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA}\)
The photo-induced dissociation was carried out by irradiating \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA}\) solution samples in acidic \(\mathrm{pH}\) (5.5) with photoirradiation \((808\ \mathrm{nm},\ 0.75\ \mathrm{W/cm}^{2})\) , followed by agarose gel electrophoresis assay and ICP to detect the release siRNA of and \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) , respectively. Briefly, \(30~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) sodium acetate \((0.01\mathrm{~M},\) \(\mathrm{pH}\,5.5)\) solution containing \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA}\) \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8}\) : \(1\ \mathrm{mg/mL})\) was irradiated with laser for different time span, and were then subjected to agarose gel electrophoresis assay ( \(1\%\) agarose gel, \(100\ \mathrm{V},\ 15\ \mathrm{min})\) . The siRNA band intensity was determined using ImageJ2x software (2.1.4.7, Rawak Software, Inc). For \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) release, \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNAs}\) samples solution after different treatments was centrifuged \((9,000\;\mathrm{rpm},5\;\mathrm{min})\) and the supernatant was collected for ICP to detect the mount of released \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) ions. The fluorescent signals of \(\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA}\) samples were recorded by a Caliper IV S Lumina II multispectral imaging system (PE, USA) using \(745\,\mathrm{\nm}\) as the excitation wavelength.
## 4.6 Cell culture
was monitored by using a \( Z\mathbf{n}^{2+} \)-selective indicator (FluoZin\(^{m-3}\), green fluorescence). Briefly, A549 cells were seeded into \( 35\;\mathrm{mm} \) glass-bottom dishes \( (5\times10^{5} \) cells/dish) and cultured in a \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) incubator in \( 37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) overnight. \( \mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA} \) nanoswitch \( (30\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}) \) was added into the dish and incubated for \( 3\;\mathrm{h} \). In control and non-irradiated group, the culture medium was replaced with the fresh medium. The irradiated group was further irradiated with laser \( 808\;\mathrm{nm} \), \( 0.75\;\mathrm{W/cm}^{2} \) for \( 5\;\mathrm{min} \). The cells were incubated with \( \mathrm{FluoZin}^{\mathrm{m-3}} \) \( (1\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}) \) in dark for \( 45\;\mathrm{min} \). Then, the cells were washed with PBS and fixed with \( 4\% \) formaldehyde for \( 10\;\mathrm{min} \) at RT, and washed with PBS. Then, the cells were imaged under confocal CLSM (Zeiss LSM 880 with Airyscan, Jena, Germany). The excitation was \( 499\;\mathrm{nm} \), and the emission filter was \( 520\;\mathrm{nm} \) (green). The images were analyzed by ImageJ \( 2\times \) software.
The endosomal/lysosomal disruption study was investigated using AO as the indicator. Briefly, A549 cells were seeded onto \( 35\;\mathrm{mm} \) glass-bottom dishes \( (5\times10^{5} \) cells/dish) and cultured in a \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) incubator in \( 37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) overnight. The \( \mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA} \) nanoswitch \( (30\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}) \) was added into the dish and incubated for \( 3\;\mathrm{h} \). A549 cells received the same different treatments as mentioned above. Subsequently, the A549 cells were stained with AO \( (5\;\upmu\mathrm{M}) \) for \( 15\;\mathrm{min} \), followed by three times wash with PBS. Finally, the cells were imaged by CLSM (Zeiss LSM 780, Jena, Germany). The excitation of AO was \( 488\;\mathrm{nm} \), and the emission was \( 525\;\mathrm{nm} \) for green, and \( 625\;\mathrm{nm} \) for red.
For siRNA intracellular trafficking, the \( \mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA} \) nanoswitch \( (30\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}) \) was added into the dish and incubated with A549 cells for \( 3\;\mathrm{h} \). After receiving the same different treatments as mentioned above, the cells were incubated with Lyso-Tracker Red \( (50\;\mathrm{nM}) \) using the protocols provided by the manufacturer (Beyotime, China), and were then fixed with \( 4\% \) formaldehyde for \( 10\;\mathrm{min} \) at RT. The cells were washed thrice with PBS, and then imaged by CLSM (Zeiss LSM 880 with Airyscan, Jena, Germany). For FAM, the excitation was \( 494\;\mathrm{nm} \), and the emission was \( 522\;\mathrm{nm} \); for the Lyso-Tracker Red, the excitation was \( 577\;\mathrm{nm} \), and the emission was at \( 590\;\mathrm{nm} \).
## 4.8 MTT assays
A549 cells were seeded on 96-well plate \( (1\,\times\,10^{4}/\mathrm{well}) \) and incubated in \( 37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) with \( 5\% \) \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) overnight. After replacing the media with the fresh one, different formulations \( (\mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8} \), PEI25k, \( \mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siRNA} \) and PEI25k@siRNA with \( \mathrm{N/P} \) ratio of 10) with different \( \mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8} \) (15, 30, 60, \( 90\;\upmu\mathrm{g/mL} \)), PEI25k (0.06, 0.12, 0.24, \( 0.36\;\upmu\mathrm{g/mL} \)) or siRNA concentrations (1.5, 3, 6, 9 pmol/mL) were added into the well and then incubated for \( 3\;\mathrm{h} \). For the control and non-irradiated group, the medium was replaced with the fresh one and further incubated for \( 12\;\mathrm{h} \); for the irradiated group, the wells were refreshed with medium and irradiated with laser \( (808\;\mathrm{nm},\,0.75\;\mathrm{W/cm}^{2},\,5\;\mathrm{min}) \), and then incubated for another \( 12\;\mathrm{h} \). The cell viabilities were evaluated by using MTT assay. PEI25k@siSur nanocomplexes with a \( \mathrm{N/P} \) ratio of 10 was used in this study, as PEI25k have optimal transfection efficiency at this \( \mathrm{N/P} \) ratio [12].
## 4.9 Synergistic effect
To investigate the synergism between photo and laser, \( f_{\mathrm{additive}} \) as well as \( f_{\mathrm{combination}} \) were determined. \( f_{\mathrm{additive}} \) is termed as \( f_{\mathrm{photo}}\times f_{\mathrm{siRNA}} \), where \( f_{\mathrm{photo}} \) is cell viability in the \( \mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8} \) plus laser group, and \( f_{\mathrm{siRNA}} \) is cell viability in the \( \mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siSur} \) group. Meanwhile, \( f_{\mathrm{combination}} \) is cell viability in the \( \mathrm{ICG@ZIF-8@siSur} \) plus laser group. When \( f_{\mathrm{combination}} \) is lower than \( f_{\mathrm{additive}} \), it indicates a synergistic effect between photo and siRNA therapy. When \( f_{\mathrm{combination}} \) equals to \( f_{\mathrm{additive}} \), there is just an additive effect. But, when \( f_{\mathrm{combination}} \) is larger than \( f_{\mathrm{additive}} \), it suggests an antagonistic effect between the two treatments.
